9. More importantly: don't lose the family members
This is the one architectural improvement I strongly recommend before you consider this feature finished.
Suppose USPTO returns:
US-A
US-B
US-C
US-D

and:
US-A ─┐
US-B ─┤
US-C ─┤── Family F123
US-D ─┘

Your ranking pipeline should only see:
Family F123 → US-A

or whichever representative your selection logic chooses.
But the UI/report should ideally be able to say:
Patent family: F123
Representative: US-A
Other family members: US-B, US-C, US-D

Otherwise you've thrown away useful patent information.
I would therefore eventually add:
family_members: dict[str, list[PatentRecord]]


to the deduplication result/metadata.
But I would not implement this yet if your immediate goal is simply getting family-aware dedup working. Get the core pipeline validated first.
10. One important caveat with your current USPTO family logic
Your current function is:
_get_continuity_family_id(raw)


and it examines:
parentContinuityBag


and chooses the earliest parent from that bag as the family root. Your code explicitly comments that the earliest parent is the family root.    Pasted markdown(2)
That is good if the ODP response gives you the complete continuity ancestry.
But consider:
Application A
     ↓
Application B
     ↓
Application C

If C only reports:
C → B

and doesn't expose:
B → A

then C gets:
family_id = B

instead of:
family_id = A

That would cause incomplete family deduplication.
So after the code changes, this is the next thing I would test against real USPTO data.
